From: use-frama-c campaign (grub-ubu, cycle-1 CEILING-LEVER)
Subject: [PATCH] cabs2cil: accept a GNU zero-length array in a NON-FINAL struct field

Frama-C 32.1 (Germanium) -- src/kernel_internals/typing/cabs2cil.ml,
                            src/kernel_services/ast_queries/cil.{ml,mli}

PROBLEM
-------
Frama-C conflates two DISTINCT constructs and applies the strict ISO C99
flexible-array-member (FAM) placement rule to both:

  (1) C99 FAM            `T x[];`   -- array with NO size (TArray(_,None))
  (2) GNU zero-length    `T x[0];`  -- array with size explicitly 0
                                       (TArray(_,Some 0))

`Cil.has_flexible_array_member` (cil.ml:6048) returns true for BOTH in
gcc/msvc mode (line 6052: `TArray (_, Some z) -> gccMode|msvcMode && isZero z`).
`Cabs2cil.addFieldInfo` (cabs2cil.ml:5134) then hard-rejects ANY field whose
type satisfies that predicate when the field is not the last one:

    non-final field `f' declared with a type containing a flexible array member.

-> `User Error` -> the whole translation unit is ABORTED before any AST is
built (exit 1), so no analysis (WP, EVA, ...) can run at all.

GCC accepts (2) in ANY field position.  Minimal reproducer, `gcc -c` OK,
`frama-c -machdep gcc_x86_64` aborts:

    struct inner { unsigned char hdr; unsigned char data[0]; } __attribute__((packed));
    struct outer { struct inner vendor; unsigned char end;  } __attribute__((packed));
    int main(void){ struct outer o; o.vendor.hdr=1; o.end=2; return o.vendor.hdr+o.end; }

THE RULE (why the two are NOT the same)
---------------------------------------
* C99 6.7.2.1p16-18: a flexible array member `T x[];` has INCOMPLETE type; the
  struct behaves "as if the member were omitted" but the member itself may not
  be used to size the object.  The standard therefore requires the FAM to be
  the LAST member, and forbids a FAM-bearing struct from being a member of
  another struct or an element of an array.  This rule is GENUINE and is kept.

* GCC manual, "Arrays of Length Zero"
  (https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Zero-Length.html): "Zero-length arrays
  are allowed in GNU C. ... they are very useful as the last element of a
  structure that is really a header for a variable-length object".  A
  zero-length array is a COMPLETE array type whose size is 0; it contributes
  zero bytes and leaves the enclosing struct COMPLETE and of well-defined size.
  Nothing about it is position-dependent, and GCC accordingly accepts a struct
  ending in a zero-length array as a member of another struct in any position
  (only `-Wpedantic` "ISO C forbids zero-size array" is emitted, for the array
  declaration itself -- never for the placement).

FIX (minimal, sound)
--------------------
Add `Cil.has_unsized_flexible_array_member`, the strict-C99 half of
`has_flexible_array_member`: identical, except that its `is_flexible_array`
recognizes ONLY `TArray(_,None)`.  Use it -- and only it -- to guard the
"must be final" error in `addFieldInfo`.

`has_flexible_array_member` itself is left BYTE-FOR-BYTE UNCHANGED, so every
other client keeps its current semantics: cil.ml:6099 (`is_complete_agg_member`),
cabs2cil.ml:3061 (FAM initializer sizing), and the `instantiate` plugin
(basic_alloc.ml, basic_blocks.ml, calloc.ml).

Behaviour matrix after the patch (`isstruct`, gcc/msvc machdep):

  last field type   position    before        after
  ---------------   ---------   -----------   -----------
  `T x[];`  (C99)   non-final   ERROR         ERROR      (unchanged, C99 kept)
  `T x[];`  (C99)   final       accepted      accepted   (unchanged)
  `T x[0];` (GNU)   non-final   ERROR         ACCEPTED   (<- the fix)
  `T x[0];` (GNU)   final       accepted      accepted   (unchanged)

Under a NON-gcc/msvc machdep nothing changes at all: `has_flexible_array_member`
already returns false for `T x[0];` there (cil.ml:6052 is gated on the mode), so
the guarded block is unreachable for zero-length arrays and the pre-existing
"declared with incomplete type" path still rejects them.

The relaxation is a pure ACCEPTANCE widening at the typing stage; it introduces
no axiom, no WP model change, and no new trusted assumption.  Layout is
computed by the existing (already zero-length-aware) sizeof/offset code.

VERIFIED (Frama-C 32.1, opam switch framac-coq8, -machdep gcc_x86_64)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Reproducer above: `frama-c` rc=1 -> rc=0, full AST printed.
2. Soundness guard -- the SAME file with `unsigned char data[];` (a true C99
   FAM) in the non-final position is STILL rejected with the identical
   "non-final field `vendor' declared with a type containing a flexible array
   member." User Error, rc=1.
3. Layout agrees with GCC: `sizeof(inner)==1`, `sizeof(outer)==2`,
   `__builtin_offsetof(outer,end)==1` accepted as `char a[cond?1:-1]` static
   asserts by BOTH gcc and the patched frama-c.
4. `frama-c -wp-detect` still lists Alt-Ergo 2.6.3, Z3 4.13.3, Coq 8.20.1.
5. Regression oracle over the grub-ubu campaign target (284 TUs, real build
   flags): 236 PARSE-OK before -> 276 after; ZERO of the previously-OK 236
   regressed.  See grub-ubu-analysis-acsl/parse-oracle-postpatch.tsv.

Filed report: bugs-to-report/frama-c-frontend-fam-non-final-field/
---
--- a/src/kernel_services/ast_queries/cil.ml
+++ b/src/kernel_services/ast_queries/cil.ml
@@ -6059,6 +6059,36 @@
     (Machine.(gccMode() || msvcMode()) && has_flexible_array_member last)
   | _ -> false
 
+(* [has_unsized_flexible_array_member t] is the strict-C99 half of
+   [has_flexible_array_member]: it recognizes ONLY a genuine C99 flexible array
+   member, i.e. a last field whose type is an array with NO size ([T x[];],
+   TArray(_,None)). It deliberately does NOT recognize a GNU *zero-length*
+   array ([T x[0];], TArray(_,Some 0)).
+
+   Rationale: the two are distinct constructs.
+   - C99 6.7.2.1: a FAM ([]) leaves the struct's size "as if the member were
+     omitted", the member itself has incomplete type, and the standard forbids
+     such a struct from being a member of another struct or an array element.
+     Hence a FAM must stay FINAL.
+   - A GNU zero-length array ([0]) is a *complete* array type of size 0
+     (GCC manual, "Arrays of Length Zero"). It contributes 0 bytes, does not
+     make the enclosing struct incomplete, and GCC therefore accepts a struct
+     ending in one as a member of another struct in ANY position.
+   Both remain gated on gcc/msvc mode by the callers, exactly as before. *)
+let rec has_unsized_flexible_array_member t =
+  let is_unsized_flexible_array t =
+    match Ast_types.unroll_skel t with
+    | TArray (_, None) -> true
+    | _ -> false
+  in
+  match Ast_types.unroll_skel t with
+  | TComp { cfields = Some ((_::_) as l) } ->
+    let last = (Extlib.last l).ftype in
+    is_unsized_flexible_array last ||
+    (Machine.(gccMode() || msvcMode()) &&
+     has_unsized_flexible_array_member last)
+  | _ -> false
+
 (* last_field is [true] if the given type is the type of the last field of
    a struct (which could be a FAM, making the whole struct complete even if
    the array type isn't. *)
--- a/src/kernel_services/ast_queries/cil.mli
+++ b/src/kernel_services/ast_queries/cil.mli
@@ -290,6 +290,21 @@
     @before 24.0-Chromium this function didn't take in account the GCC/MSVC mode
 *)
 
+(** [true] iff the given type is a [struct] whose last field is a *genuine C99*
+    flexible array member, i.e. an array declared with no size ([T x[];]).
+    Unlike {!has_flexible_array_member}, a GNU zero-length array ([T x[0];]) is
+    NOT considered a flexible array member here: it is a complete array type of
+    size 0 (GCC manual, "Arrays of Length Zero") which contributes no bytes and
+    does not make the enclosing struct incomplete, so GCC accepts it in any
+    field position. In GCC/MSVC mode this function recursively searches the
+    type of the last field, as {!has_flexible_array_member} does.
+
+    Use this (rather than {!has_flexible_array_member}) when enforcing the C99
+    rule that a FAM-bearing struct may only appear as the LAST member of an
+    enclosing struct.
+*)
+val has_unsized_flexible_array_member: typ -> bool
+
 (** returns the type of the result of an arithmetic operator applied to
     values of the corresponding input types.
     @since Nitrogen-20111001 (moved from Cabs2cil)
--- a/src/kernel_internals/typing/cabs2cil.ml
+++ b/src/kernel_internals/typing/cabs2cil.ml
@@ -5132,7 +5132,17 @@
         Kernel.error ~source
           "field `%s' declared as a function" n
       else if Cil.has_flexible_array_member ftype && isstruct then begin
-        if not (last_group && last_field) then
+        (* C99 6.7.2.1: a struct ending in a genuine flexible array member
+           ([T x[];], i.e. an array with NO size) may only appear as the LAST
+           member of an enclosing struct -- the FAM has incomplete type.
+           A GNU *zero-length* array ([T x[0];]) is a different construct: a
+           COMPLETE array type of size 0 (GCC manual, "Arrays of Length Zero")
+           that contributes no bytes and leaves the enclosing struct complete,
+           so GCC accepts it in ANY field position. Restrict the "must be
+           final" rule to the genuine (unsized) FAM; keep rejecting the latter
+           exactly as before. *)
+        if not (last_group && last_field)
+        && Cil.has_unsized_flexible_array_member ftype then
           Kernel.error ~source
             "non-final field `%s' declared with a type containing a flexible \
              array member."
